hat is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T19:31:59-08:00","id":1043317,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1007357-inspiration-for-werewolf?p=1043313#post1043313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's just ... odd. And still sounds like a better idea than the movie I was thinking of.</div> </div> </div> </div>but look how brutal!<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://i1248.photobucket.com/albums/hh497/ALRO00/BadMoon3_zpspipzczgz.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12902-wisdominchains","name":"WISDOMINCHAINS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12902_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"139"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T07:30:33-08:00","id":1043534,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1007357-inspiration-for-werewolf?p=1043313#post1043313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's just ... odd. And still sounds like a better idea than the movie I was thinking of.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is based on a children's book, I believe. But the interaction between the dog and the werewolf is at times pretty silly. Like a literal pissing contest.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T08:37:05-08:00","id":1043573,"title":"","content":"There is NO better inspiration for the slow, painful and humiliating transformation from a human being to a sickly and pathetic formori committed to film than David Cronenberg's &quot;The Fly&quot; (1986)<br /> <br /> Jeff Goldblum's tour de force performance, most grossly denied the Oscar it deserved, is a haunting study of grief, anger denial and acceptance that afflicts one possessed by an incurable disease; not unlike the stages of acceptance that all formerly-human World of Darkness Denizens would experience, Formori or not, when you think about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4684-lin-liren","name":"Lin Liren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T17:53:17-08:00","id":1043788,"title":"","content":"A lot of Cronenberg's stuff is great for WoD inspiration. Spider, Naked Lunch, The Dead Zone, eXistenZ, Scanners, Videodrome ....<br /> <br /> Back when the revamped World of Darkness was being published, I kept hoping for a WoD: Dark Science book that would cover the vein of horror and suspense fiction that included The Fly remake, as well as stuff like Coma, The Stepford Wives (the book is way creepier than the farce the franchise turned into), and the like. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T19:40:11-08:00","id":1043819,"title":"","content":"It's VERY easy to forget that Garou are &quot;monsters&quot; because of their Honorable Warrior Culture and Unambiguously Noble Goals.<br /> <br /> And just then, Cinemax Taiwan starts to show THE Finest example of what it means to be a &quot;Heroic Monster&quot;<br /> <br /> Tim Burton's &quot;Batman&quot; (1989)<br /> <br /> Morso than ANY Film made since, Michael Keaton's Bruce Wayne encapsulates the TRUE spirit of what it means to be Batman:<br /> <br /> A horrifying <b>monster</b> and demon that strikes the fear of death itself into the hearts of evil, willing to KILL (yes, KILL) if necessary to protect the remaining decency, innocence and goodness that is left in this world.<br /> <br /> What Batman stands for is best summarized by tearful, hysterical testament screamed to the police by the thugs that terrorized the innocent family in the film's opening:<br /> <br /> &quot;I'm Telling Ya Man! A Giant Bat!!&quot;<br /> <br /> An Unambiguously Heroic Monster, But a <b>Monster</b> all the same.<br /> <br /> Not unlike the Garou. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4684,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4684-lin-liren","user":"Lin Liren","date":". 02-04-2017, 11:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4684-lin-liren","name":"Lin Liren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-12T14:26:44-08:00","id":1047886,"title":"","content":"This weekend I watched something called &quot;Late Phases&quot; on Netflix. It's not particularly brilliant and the werewolf designs are almost laughable, but the main story and the acting are pretty good. Potentially good inspiration for a werewolf hunter character, perhaps.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T06:35:34-08:00","id":1048210,"title":"","content":"Besides <b>Aliens (1986)</b> , one of the most atmospheric depictions of what it would feel like to descend into the subterranean hellscape that is the Black Spiral Dancer hive would be Del Torro's <b>Mimic (1997)</b><br /> <br /> An endless yet claustrophobic abyss of shadows and crumbling glory of centuries past, with hissing and gibbering clawed death scuttling through the shadows? Yeah, that pretty much summarizes a Black Spiral Hive (with Formori squatters) down just right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4684-lin-liren","name":"Lin Liren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T16:39:47-08:00","id":1048472,"title":"","content":"On that note: Which would emerge triumphant should there be an &quot;Ultimate Clash?&quot;<br /> <br /> A pack of Black Spiral Dancers or The 150 odd strong hive of acid blooded parasitic beasts that infested the &quot;Hadley's Hope&quot; Colony on Archeron in <b>Aliens (1986)?</b> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Does Acid blood count as Lethal or Aggravated Damage? Would a Klaive, imbued with spirit power, be able to survive acid blood?<br /> <br /> Also: would the birth of a Chestburster count as Lethal or Aggravated Damage? With his regeneration, a Garou can no doubt survive the process compared to a homid, right?<br /> <br /> And on <i>that</i> note: Alongside its Gorgeous Gothic Visuals, <b>Alien 3 (1992)</b> has been <i>the</i> film that I think of when it comes to imagery of &quot;being pursued by a blindingly fast beast that can kill you in one hit.&quot;<br /> <br /> Better yet? It allows you to see what said life-and-death-pursuit would look like from the perspective of the prey AND the predator. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4684,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4684-lin-liren","user":"Lin Liren","date":". 02-13-2017, 08:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4684-lin-liren","name":"Lin Liren","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"202"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-13T17:12:30-08:00","id":1048488,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lin Liren</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1007357-inspiration-for-werewolf?p=1048210#post1048210\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides <b>Aliens (1986)</b> , one of the most atmospheric depictions of what it would feel like to descend into the subterranean hellscape that is the Black Spiral Dancer hive would be Del Torro's <b>Mimic (1997)</b></div> </div> </div> </div>I'd forgotten that Mimic was one of his films. <br /> <br /> The Descent (2005) also works well for that. <br /> <br /> I'd also recommend the novel Reliquary by Douglas Preston and Lincoln Child. It's a sequel to their earlier book Relic (basis for the 1997 movie), involving the massive labyrinths of tunnels and caves under Manhattan. (Both books really are good World of Darkness reading in general.)<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-14T15:54:38-08:00","id":1048980,"title":"","content":"Back with some more resources: I feel the need to sing the praises of both a) the works of folklorists and b) the works of historians on 'real-life' werewolves in culture!<br /> <br /> My current chronicle takes place in Australia, an interesting setting that doesn't possess the readily-available online resources on indigenous mythology that, say, the US and Canada has. I've been spending a fair amount of time at the state library chasing brief footnotes on spirits that seem to fit what I'm attempting to do with the setting, hah. The most useful work I've found thus far is Berndt &amp; Berndt's (a married couple) <i>The Speaking Land</i>, a detailed exploration of Aboriginal culture and religion, but the creative Storyteller can find much to work with in Davey's <i>A Guide to Australian Folklore </i>and her earlier <i>Oxford Companion to Australian Folklore</i>. An illustrated collection of stories that I grew up with, Mountford's <i>The Dawn of Time</i> (printed outside of Aus as <i>The Dreamtime</i>), is also worth tracking down. <br /> <br /> For non-Australians, I (re)direct everyone's attention to that archetypal work on modern, urban, spontaneously-generated folklore, '<a href=\"http://www.miaminewtimes.com/news/myths-over-miami-6393117\" target=\"_blank\">Myths Over Miami</a>'. Those who have never read this article--and it's not too long--should check it out for inspiration and to get an idea of how urban folklore works. <br /> <br /> Now onto the good stuff: academic works on werewolves. These make for interesting inspiration; they often can't help you directly, as the werewolves of folklore are very different to the Garou, but again, the enterprising ST can find some good stuff here. A short list of what I consider to be the 'core' academic werewolf texts: Montague's <i>The Werewolf in Lore and Legend</i>, Otten's <i>A Lycanthropy Reader</i>, Coudray's <i>The Curse of the Werewolf</i>, and Sconduto's <i>Metamorphoses of the Werewolf</i>. I also recommend Carrington's very famous work <i>The Dream Hunters of Corsica</i>; which, while not about werewolves, details the remains of a pre-Christian religion in Corsica, whose adherents believe they are 'dream hunters', folk healers who protect their people from dark spirits. A related work--this time on the<i> benandanti</i> of 16th-century Italy, who said they travelled at night in the shape of animals to battle evil--is Ginzburg's <i>The Night Battles</i>. <br /> <br /> There is also the only (?) academic paper on WtA that I know of, Cate's 'Participatory lycanthropy: female werewolves in Werewolf: The Apocalypse', in <i>She-Wolf: A Cultural History of Female Werewolves</i>, quite a recent collection. I've not been able to find a copy at my university; I've strongly considered requesting it just to give it a look, though :P If anyone's read it, I'd love to know their thoughts!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12920-anomalocaris","name":"anomalocaris","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-15T19:14:06-08:00","id":1049651,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1007357-inspiration-for-werewolf?p=1048488#post1048488\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd forgotten that Mimic was one of his films.<br /> <br /> The Descent (2005) also works well for that.<br /> <br /> I'd also recommend the novel Reliquary by Douglas Preston and Lincoln Child. It's a sequel to their earlier book Relic (basis for the 1997 movie), involving the massive labyrinths of tunnels and caves under Manhattan. (Both books really are good World of Darkness reading in general.)<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bI was just about to mention The Descent. <a href=\"http://www.imdb.com/title/tt0402901/?ref_=fn_al_tt_1\" target=\"_blank\"><i>The Cave</i></a>(2005) kind of blends aspects of <i>The Fly</i> with those of <i>The Descent</i>; in that the protagonists are not only being attacked by monsters, but are slowly being turned into monsters themselves...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/12.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div> &quot;A free society is one in which it is safe to be unpopular.&quot;<br /> <span style=\"color:DarkRed\"><span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Comic Sans MS\">I find <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=3&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwin4NXQzLfXAhVGySYKHV3TDXYQFgg3MAI&amp;url=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.ign.com%2Farticles%2F2017%2F04%2F25%2Fmarvels-nick-spencer-on-captain-americas-betrayal-and-handling-fan-reactions&amp;usg=AOvVaw2EcsX7n5ebntygb0TizyGl\" target=\"_blank\">Nick Spencer</a> and <a href=\"https://www.google.com/url?sa=t&amp;rct=j&amp;q=&amp;esrc=s&amp;source=web&amp;cd=4&amp;cad=rja&amp;uact=8&amp;ved=0ahUKEwiK7d7szLfXAhXCWSYKHTpKBbcQFgg4MAM&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fen.wikipedia.org%2Fwiki%2FRichard_B._Spencer&amp;usg=AOvVaw1tuP6phfINia-bh_jWsDT_\" target=\"_blank\">Richard Spencer</a> to be equally repulsive.</span></span></span>","edited":{"userid":6138,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","user":"Mangle77","date":". 02-16-2017, 01:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6138-mangle77","name":"Mangle77","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6138_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"605"}